## Freeing the Parrot 2.0 — Execution & Deployment Report

1. Project Overview

Freeing the Parrot 2.0 (FTP 2.0) is an interactive research apparatus that guides a participant through a conversation, observes interaction signals, generates post-session interpretations, presents 27 reflective readings, allows participant selection, and concludes with a machine-readable reflection.
The system is designed to produce readings, not rewards or diagnoses.
The project currently consists of:
- A frontend experience in frontend/kimi
- A Python backend in ftp_backend_service.py
- Supporting modules in ftp/
- A Navarasa analysis engine
- A session lifecycle and state machine
- Evidence and interaction-profile pipelines
- A post-session reading and 27-card deck pipeline
- Data Showdown / Data Journey stages
- Participant selection and resonance interaction
- Final machine reveal and closing stages
- Vercel deployment for the frontend
- Render deployment for the backend
- Optional persistence infrastructure

2. Core Experience Flow

The intended FTP 2.0 journey is:
MULTIMODAL INPUT
        ↓
EVENT NORMALIZATION
        ↓
EVENT STORE
        ↓
LIVE PARROT
        ↓
SILENT READER
        ↓
"I AM DONE!"
        ↓
SESSION LOCK
        ↓
POST-SESSION INTERPRETATION
        ↓
27 QUALITATIVE READINGS
        ↓
PARTICIPANT SELECTION
        ↓
DATA REVEAL
        ↓
INTERACTION PROFILE
        ↓
WHAT THE SYSTEM CANNOT KNOW
        ↓
CONSENT / PURGE

The central architectural principle is:
The Parrot Remains Ignorant.

The live Parrot does not receive the Silent Reader's telemetry or post-session analytical interpretation during the conversation.
The participant-facing reading layer is also separated from the internal analytical layer.

3. Deployment Architecture

FTP 2.0 is deployed as two primary services:
                    PARTICIPANT
                         │
                         ▼
              ┌─────────────────────┐
              │       VERCEL        │
              │   FTP 2.0 Frontend  │
              └──────────┬──────────┘
                         │
                         │ API requests
                         ▼
              ┌─────────────────────┐
              │       RENDER        │
              │   FTP 2.0 Backend   │
              └──────────┬──────────┘
                         │
              ┌──────────┴──────────┐
              │                     │
              ▼                     ▼
        Session / Analysis    Optional Persistence
              │
              ▼
        Silent Reader
              │
              ▼
       Reading Pipeline
              │
              ▼
          27 Cards
              │
              ▼
        Data Reveal

The public frontend is hosted through Vercel.
The backend is hosted through Render at:
https://ftp2-backend.onrender.com

The production frontend is served through the Vercel deployment associated with the FTP_2.0 branch.

4. Major Failure States Encountered

4.1 Backend Unavailable — “Failed to Fetch”
The browser initially displayed:
The apparatus could not reach its backend. Please try again.

The frontend was unable to complete requests to the backend.
The configured backend URL was subsequently confirmed as:

https://ftp2-backend.onrender.com

An additional issue was identified around optional persistence. A persistence failure could occur while the live session itself was otherwise usable.
The system was subsequently adjusted so that optional persistence is treated as best-effort rather than allowing a temporary persistence failure to immediately destroy the live participant experience.

5. Vercel 404

The public site initially displayed:
404 NOT_FOUND

Cause
The frontend was being built into:
kimi_dist

while Vercel was configured to look for:
dist

As a result, the source could build successfully while the deployed site still had no published entry point.
The output directory was corrected so that Vercel serves:
kimi_dist

Frontend Dependency Issue
A second deployment problem was discovered.
Vercel installed the root project's dependencies but did not automatically install the separate frontend dependencies contained within:
frontend/kimi

The frontend required dependencies such as Vite and its React plugin.
The root build process was therefore updated so that frontend dependencies are installed before the frontend production build is executed.
Branch Alignment Issue
A further deployment lesson emerged:
A successful deployment of a v0/... branch does not mean that the production URL has received that change.
The production branch must contain the fix.
For FTP 2.0, the production branch is:
FTP_2.0

6. Lifecycle 404 / Unknown POST Route

A backend lifecycle issue was discovered around:
input_complete

and:
card_selection

The backend correctly processed the requested state transition.
However, those branches did not return a success response.
Execution therefore continued into the generic unknown-route response.
This produced a 404 even though the underlying action had actually succeeded.
Fix
Explicit JSON success responses were added immediately after both lifecycle actions.
The backend now returns a successful response after completing these actions rather than falling through to the generic 404 handler.

7. Participant-Facing Card Spoiler Validation Failure

During post-session interpretation, the frontend displayed:
Internal FTP error:
participant-facing card text contains analytical spoilers

This was traced to the reading-deck generation pipeline.
The relevant sequence was:
LIVE CONVERSATION
        ↓
I AM DONE
        ↓
SESSION LOCK
        ↓
POST-SESSION INTERPRETATION
        ↓
READING PROFILE
        ↓
READING SEED
        ↓
27-CARD COMPOSITION
        ↓
DECK VALIDATION

The validator correctly rejected a participant-facing card when analytical or internal system language appeared in the generated reading.
Examples of prohibited material included:
- evidence IDs
- inference IDs
- event IDs
- confidence
- telemetry
- Gemini
- Navarasa labels
- analytical wording
- internal system terminology
Corrective Approach
The validator itself was not weakened.
Instead, the reading-seed sanitization layer was strengthened.
The principle is:
INTERNAL ANALYTICAL INFORMATION
                ↓
        SANITIZATION BOUNDARY
                ↓
PARTICIPANT-SAFE QUALITATIVE READING

This preserves the original analytical information internally as provenance while preventing it from leaking into participant-facing prose.
The issue was intermittent because the analytical material produced during different conversations could vary. A given session might generate a safe seed, while another session could produce language containing one of the validator's prohibited terms.
The final reproduction condition was identified as:
ENGAGED CONVERSATION
        ↓
I AM DONE
        ↓
POST-SESSION READING GENERATION
        ↓
CARD VALIDATION
        ↓
POSSIBLE SPOILER REJECTION

This isolated the problem to the transition between analytical interpretation and participant-facing reading composition.

8. Navarasa Engagement Detection
Another issue appeared during engaged conversations.
The Data Showdown could display:
NO RASA DETECTED

even when the participant was clearly engaged.
Cause
The original Navarasa engine relied heavily on a narrow vocabulary of explicit emotional words and phrases.
Natural engagement expressions such as:
tell me more
I want to understand
I am interested
this matters to me

could therefore be treated as ordinary text.
The system could subsequently fall back to:
Shanta
no_emotion_detected

even though the interaction contained meaningful curiosity or engagement.
Fix
Engagement and curiosity signals were added to the existing:
Adbhuta

category.
Additional expressions included:
- “tell me more”
- “keep going”
- “I want to understand”
- “I want to know”
- “this matters to me”
Engaged examples were then verified to produce Adbhuta rather than no_emotion_detected.

9. Git Push Failure

During development, the system reported:
There was an error auto committing and pushing changes:
Unknown error.

The local work remained preserved in:
f31966e

on:
v0/session-archetype

Manual push attempts subsequently encountered a GitHub server-side Internal Server Error.
This was treated as a GitHub/push-service issue rather than a code failure.
No destructive reset, deletion, or repository reconstruction was performed.
The important recovery principle was:
FAILED PUSH
     ↓
CHECK LOCAL COMMIT
     ↓
CHECK REMOTE STATE
     ↓
PRESERVE WORK
     ↓
RETRY / MERGE

10. Reliability and Persistence Changes

Changes were made to make optional persistence best-effort.
The system was adjusted so that:
- A temporary persistence failure does not necessarily terminate the live session.
- Session creation can fall back safely when optional persistence is unavailable.
- The participant can continue interacting with the live system when persistence is temporarily unavailable.
- Frontend server-error messaging is clearer.
Commit:
a844d93

Branch:
v0/prevent-ftp-runtime-failures

11. Reading Seed Privacy and Spoiler Protection

The reading pipeline was strengthened to ensure that internal analytical material cannot accidentally become participant-facing prose.
Changes included:
- Participant-facing reading-seed sanitization.
- Detection of internal identifiers.
- Detection of analytical terminology.
- Safe fallback prose.
- Preservation of original claims internally.
- Preservation of interpretation data internally.
- Preservation of confidence and evidence references internally.
- Regression tests covering analytical and internal terms.
Commits included:
18462f7

Branch:
v0/sanitize-reading-seeds

and:
3380287

Branch:
v0/harden-reading-seed-sanitizer

