20. Deployment Alignment

One of the most important operational lessons from the project is that a successful development branch is not necessarily the production deployment.
The deployment chain must remain aligned:
GitHub
   ↓
FTP_2.0
   ↓
Vercel Production

and:
GitHub
   ↓
FTP_2.0
   ↓
Render Backend

A fix existing only on a v0/... branch does not affect the public experience until it is merged into the branch used for production deployment.

21. Working With Future Failures

If a professor, reviewer, or participant encounters an unexpected error in the public deployment, the appropriate response is not to immediately reset or rebuild the repository.
The recommended diagnostic sequence is:
SCREENSHOT / ERROR
        ↓
IDENTIFY EXACT ACTION
        ↓
REPRODUCE
        ↓
CHECK FRONTEND
        ↓
CHECK BACKEND
        ↓
CHECK RENDER LOGS
        ↓
CHECK GITHUB COMMIT
        ↓
ISOLATE ROOT CAUSE
        ↓
MAKE SMALLEST FIX
        ↓
RUN TESTS
        ↓
DEPLOY
        ↓
VERIFY PUBLIC URL

For an external reviewer, the useful information to collect is:
1. A screenshot of the error.
2. The approximate time it occurred.
3. The action immediately preceding the error.
4. Whether the issue occurred again after restarting the session.
This is sufficient to distinguish most frontend, backend, deployment, and application-logic failures.

22. Known Limitations

FTP 2.0 is an experimental research prototype, not a production-grade commercial system.
The system consists of multiple services and analytical stages, so transient infrastructure or runtime failures remain possible.
The intended experience is:
INPUT
  ↓
LIVE CONVERSATION
  ↓
I AM DONE
  ↓
SESSION LOCK
  ↓
POST-SESSION INTERPRETATION
  ↓
READING DECK
  ↓
PARTICIPANT SELECTION
  ↓
DATA REVEAL
  ↓
REFLECTION

If a failure occurs during a post-session stage, restarting the session may be required.
The system should not be interpreted as:
- a psychological assessment
- a diagnostic tool
- a permanent personality profiler
- an objective statement of who the participant is
Its readings are generated within the context of a single interaction.

23. Important Engineering Lessons

23.1 Local success does not prove production success
A successful local build does not prove that production is running that same build.
23.2 Production branch alignment matters
The branch containing the fix must match the branch used by the production deployment.
For FTP 2.0:
FTP_2.0

23.3 Build output configuration must match
The build output folder must exactly match Vercel's configured output directory.
FTP 2.0 uses:
kimi_dist

23.4 Backend state changes require responses
A backend operation can successfully change the server-side state and still appear broken to the browser if the server does not return a successful response.
23.5 Analytical data must remain separated from participant-facing language
Internal evidence, inference, telemetry, identifiers, confidence values, and analytical terminology must not accidentally become participant-facing prose.
23.6 Validators can be safety mechanisms
A validator rejecting a reading deck is not necessarily a system failure.
In FTP 2.0, the validator exists partly to prevent internal analytical information from leaking into the participant-facing experience.
23.7 Failed Git pushes do not necessarily mean lost work
When GitHub reports a server-side push failure, the correct response is to inspect the local commit and remote branch before performing any destructive operation.
The local repository can still contain the complete work.
23.8 Recovery should be controlled
The preferred recovery pattern is:
INSPECT
   ↓
TEST
   ↓
PRESERVE
   ↓
MERGE
   ↓
DEPLOY
   ↓
VERIFY

rather than:
ERROR
   ↓
RESET
   ↓
DELETE
   ↓
REBUILD EVERYTHING

24. Final Status

FTP 2.0 has progressed from a locally developed prototype through iterative debugging, testing, deployment, recovery, and public validation into a functioning deployed research apparatus.
The most significant technical achievement is not simply that the application runs.
It is that the system now maintains a deliberate separation between:
WHAT THE PARTICIPANT SAYS
        ↓
WHAT THE SYSTEM OBSERVES
        ↓
WHAT THE SYSTEM MEASURES
        ↓
WHAT THE SYSTEM INTERPRETS
        ↓
WHAT THE SYSTEM CONSTRUCTS
        ↓
WHAT THE PARTICIPANT SELECTS
        ↓
WHAT THE SYSTEM CAN AND CANNOT KNOW

This separation is central to the conceptual and technical integrity of Freeing the Parrot 2.0.
The system is therefore best understood not as an AI that claims to know the participant, but as an experimental apparatus that makes visible how an interaction can be transformed into machine-readable signals, interpretations, selections, and ultimately a representation of the person who interacted with it.
